1niw|n|One who, or that which, propels. ; A contrivance for propelling a steam vessel, usually consisting of a screw placed in the stern under water, and made to revolve by an engine; a propeller wheel. ; A steamboat thus propelled; a screw steamer.	N:86l,112,2~86l,112,2
1nj9|n|Belonging to one; one's own; individual. ; Belonging to the natural or essential constitution; peculiar; not common; particular; as, every animal has his proper instincts and appetites. ; Befitting one's nature, qualities, etc.; suitable in all respect; appropriate; right; fit; decent; as, water is the proper element for fish; a proper dress. ; Becoming in appearance; well formed; handsome. ; Pertaining to one of a species, but not common to the whole; not appellative; -- opposed to common; as, a proper name; Dublin is the proper name of a city. ; Rightly so called; strictly considered; as, Greece proper; the garden proper. ; Represented in its natural color; -- said of any object used as a charge.	V:16xg,10,2~16xg,10,2	N:5ji,40,1;200c,39,1;24mx,20,2;d73,10,1;1vkc,4,3~1vkc,4,3;24mx,20,2;5ji,40,1;200c,39,1;d73,10,1
